@page "/built-on-it"

<PageTitle>Built on it · louis-agent</PageTitle>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">Built on it</span>
    <h2 class="chrome">Two agents run on the engine today.</h2>
    <div class="grid-2">
        <article class="panel">
            <h3>Puls</h3>
            <p>A general assistant in everyday South African Afrikaans (English too). It doesn't answer with tools itself: it hands each task to a specialist, a second session with only the tools that task needs and a role that says how to work and report back. The work shows live, with the sources it read and what it cost, in rand.</p>
            <p class="muted">Five specialists today: a researcher, a reader, a historian, an analyst and a cook. A shops pack reads a retailer's prices and deals from its own catalogue.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel">
            <h3>The coding agent</h3>
            <p>Works on your repository with real tools: files, git, dotnet, Python, PowerShell, bash and web search. It runs inside Rider through the Agent Client Protocol, in a web app with a changes view and a branches view, in the terminal, over the HTTP API, and its tools alone over MCP. Pushing always stays with you.</p>
            <p class="muted">A proof of concept beside it: an orchestrator that reads a service's errors and has the coding agent fix and verify them.</p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="band">
    <span class="kicker">Hosts</span>
    <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel small"><h4>Rider</h4><p>An Agent Client Protocol server over stdio for Rider's AI chat.</p></div>
        <div class="panel small"><h4>Web app and HTTP API</h4><p>Sessions with answers streamed as server-sent events; the API serves the web app.</p></div>
        <div class="panel small"><h4>CLI and MCP server</h4><p>Interactive or one-shot prompts in the terminal; the tools handed to any MCP client, which brings its own model.</p></div>
    </div>
</section>
